WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.863
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.163 --> 00:00:08.224
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.524 --> 00:00:16.795
Unit fifty-six: Technology and Employment. In this unit, you will discuss how automation is changing work.

4
00:00:17.783 --> 00:00:19.899
technological unemployment.

5
00:00:20.599 --> 00:00:25.431
technological unemployment means job loss caused by new technology.

6
00:00:25.731 --> 00:00:30.750
Economists debate whether technological unemployment is permanent.

7
00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:35.611
Your turn. Say: technological unemployment.

8
00:00:38.311 --> 00:00:40.195
technological unemployment.

9
00:00:41.183 --> 00:00:42.126
reskilling.

10
00:00:42.826 --> 00:00:46.324
reskilling means learning new skills for a different job.

11
00:00:46.624 --> 00:00:50.353
Reskilling programmes help factory workers move into IT.

12
00:00:51.053 --> 00:00:54.216
Your turn. Say: reskilling.

13
00:00:56.916 --> 00:00:57.871
reskilling.

14
00:00:58.883 --> 00:01:00.778
human-machine collaboration.

15
00:01:01.478 --> 00:01:05.568
human-machine collaboration means people and machines working together.

16
00:01:05.868 --> 00:01:09.156
Human-machine collaboration can increase productivity.

17
00:01:09.856 --> 00:01:13.797
Your turn. Say: human-machine collaboration.

18
00:01:16.497 --> 00:01:18.334
human-machine collaboration.

19
00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:21.210
algorithmic management.

20
00:01:21.910 --> 00:01:26.766
algorithmic management means using software to assign and evaluate work.

21
00:01:27.066 --> 00:01:30.575
Delivery riders are controlled by algorithmic management.

22
00:01:31.275 --> 00:01:35.460
Your turn. Say: algorithmic management.

23
00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:39.799
algorithmic management.

24
00:01:40.783 --> 00:01:41.924
digital divide.

25
00:01:42.624 --> 00:01:47.630
digital divide means the gap between people with and without access to technology.

26
00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:50.673
The digital divide affects rural students.

27
00:01:51.373 --> 00:01:54.501
Your turn. Say: digital divide.

28
00:01:57.201 --> 00:01:58.434
digital divide.

29
00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:01.368
labour market polarisation.

30
00:02:02.068 --> 00:02:08.096
labour market polarisation means growth of high- and low-skilled jobs while middle jobs disappear.

31
00:02:08.396 --> 00:02:11.882
Labour market polarisation increases inequality.

32
00:02:12.582 --> 00:02:15.943
Your turn. Say: labour market polarisation.

33
00:02:18.643 --> 00:02:20.561
labour market polarisation.

34
00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:22.969
automation wave.

35
00:02:23.669 --> 00:02:28.350
automation wave means a period when many tasks become automated.

36
00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.718
The next automation wave may affect office jobs.

37
00:02:32.418 --> 00:02:35.825
Your turn. Say: automation wave.

38
00:02:38.525 --> 00:02:39.991
automation wave.

39
00:02:40.983 --> 00:02:42.298
future skills.

40
00:02:42.998 --> 00:02:48.248
future skills means abilities that will be valuable in the future job market.

41
00:02:48.548 --> 00:02:52.417
Creativity and adaptability are key future skills.

42
00:02:53.117 --> 00:02:56.396
Your turn. Say: future skills.

43
00:02:59.096 --> 00:03:00.457
future skills.

44
00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:03.705
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:04.005 --> 00:03:11.101
Ms. Sarah, if robots had been cheaper twenty years ago, would Cambodia's factories still exist today?

46
00:03:12.417 --> 00:03:17.019
Perhaps not as many. Low labour costs gave the country an advantage.

47
00:03:18.317 --> 00:03:21.419
And now the next automation wave is coming.

48
00:03:22.717 --> 00:03:28.190
Yes. If governments had planned reskilling earlier, workers would feel more secure now.

49
00:03:29.483 --> 00:03:31.703
Will machines replace teachers too?

50
00:03:33.017 --> 00:03:38.142
Unlikely. The future is human-machine collaboration, not replacement.

51
00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:42.599
But the digital divide could leave rural people behind.

52
00:03:43.883 --> 00:03:50.437
That's the biggest risk. If I were a policymaker, I'd have invested in rural internet years ago.

53
00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:54.992
Grammar focus: Mixed conditionals (mastery).

54
00:03:55.292 --> 00:04:00.287
Mastering mixed conditionals means moving smoothly between time frames.

55
00:04:00.587 --> 00:04:04.049
If robots had been cheaper, factories wouldn't exist today.

56
00:04:04.349 --> 00:04:08.055
If I were a policymaker, I'd have invested years ago.

57
00:04:08.355 --> 00:04:13.617
Mastery means switching naturally between time frames to analyse cause and effect.

58
00:04:14.617 --> 00:04:17.873
Now read and listen. Will a Robot Take Your Job?.

59
00:04:18.173 --> 00:04:22.796
Every major technological change has destroyed some jobs and created others.

60
00:04:24.083 --> 00:04:28.324
Economists disagree about whether the current automation wave will be different.

61
00:04:29.617 --> 00:04:34.287
Routine tasks, both manual and clerical, are the most vulnerable.

62
00:04:35.583 --> 00:04:40.776
This has led to labour market polarisation, with growth in high- and low-paid jobs.

63
00:04:42.083 --> 00:04:46.393
Algorithmic management is already shaping the working lives of millions.

64
00:04:47.683 --> 00:04:53.270
If workers had been offered reskilling earlier, many transitions would be smoother today.

65
00:04:54.583 --> 00:05:01.076
Experts emphasise future skills such as creativity, communication and adaptability.

66
00:05:02.383 --> 00:05:08.690
Closing the digital divide may determine who benefits from technology and who is left behind.

67
00:05:09.983 --> 00:05:12.601
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:12.901 --> 00:05:16.514
Job loss caused by new technology is blank.

69
00:05:16.814 --> 00:05:22.848
A, technological unemployment. B, reskilling. C, digital divide.

70
00:05:26.850 --> 00:05:30.394
The answer is A: technological unemployment.

71
00:05:31.883 --> 00:05:34.928
If I had learned coding, I blank a better job now.

72
00:05:35.228 --> 00:05:39.288
A, would have. B, would have had. C, will have.

73
00:05:43.283 --> 00:05:45.863
The answer is A: would have.

74
00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:50.627
If she were more patient, she blank that argument.

75
00:05:50.927 --> 00:05:55.358
A, wouldn't start. B, wouldn't have started. C, won't start.

76
00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:02.151
The answer is B: wouldn't have started.

77
00:06:03.650 --> 00:06:07.565
The gap between people with and without technology is the blank.

78
00:06:07.865 --> 00:06:13.016
A, digital divide. B, automation wave. C, future skills.

79
00:06:17.017 --> 00:06:19.782
The answer is A: digital divide.

80
00:06:21.283 --> 00:06:24.955
According to the text, which tasks are most vulnerable?

81
00:06:25.255 --> 00:06:31.486
A, creative tasks. B, routine tasks. C, caring tasks.

82
00:06:35.483 --> 00:06:38.191
The answer is B: routine tasks.

83
00:06:39.683 --> 00:06:45.087
Now it's your turn. Part one: How should young Cambodians prepare for automation?

84
00:06:50.387 --> 00:06:55.847
Part two: Use a mixed conditional, recommend two future skills and explain why.

85
00:07:02.147 --> 00:07:17.120
Young people should develop digital literacy and communication skills. If my school had taught coding, I would feel more confident now. These skills matter because machines struggle to replace human creativity and empathy.

86
00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:19.489
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:20.483 --> 00:07:25.318
Great work today! You learned eight new words and mixed conditionals (mastery).

88
00:07:25.618 --> 00:07:29.768
Today's value is being prepared. Get ready before you go.

89
00:07:30.068 --> 00:07:33.742
See you in Unit fifty-seven. Goodbye!

90
00:07:34.042 --> 00:07:36.834
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

